Title:
Phantom in the Ring: BYOVD, IOCTL Hunting, and the Art of Killing EDRs from Kernel Space

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post breaks down an end-to-end Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver (BYOVD) workflow to terminate EDR/AV agents from kernel space using a legitimately signed third-party driver.  
- It focuses on discovering dangerous IOCTL surfaces (e.g., process-kill primitives) and exploiting them via `DeviceIoControl` to bypass Protected Process Light (PPL) and tamper protection without disabling DSE or loading unsigned code.  
- A concrete case study uses Lenovo’s signed `BootRepair.sys`, highlighting a zero-DACL device object and an ungated IOCTL (`0x222014`) that takes a PID and calls `PsLookupProcessByProcessId` → `ObOpenObjectByPointer` → `ZwTerminateProcess`.  
- The article also covers scalable triage (LOLDrivers + automated tooling like DrvEye), IOCTL reverse engineering in IDA/Ghidra, and dynamic IOCTL interception with Frida.  
- Defensive value is emphasized via a hunting/detection playbook (Sysmon EID 6 driver loads, Service Control Manager 7045 kernel service creation, anomalous `\\.\Device` opens + `DeviceIoControl`) and discussion of HVCI/WDAC blocklist gaps and ingest-latency constraints.  
- Useful for red teams/pentesters building kernel-assisted defense evasion, and for blue teams engineering detections and hardening (HVCI/WDAC + LOLDrivers-based deny policies).

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD (signed vulnerable driver abuse) and kernel-mode process termination primitives
- IOCTL discovery/reversing (IRP dispatch, `IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL`, `CTL_CODE`, access/method fields)
- PPL/tamper-protection bypass via kernel handle creation (`ObOpenObjectByPointer`)
- Driver loading via SCM (`sc.exe` kernel services) and device object security (NULL SecurityDescriptor / missing DACL)
- Detection engineering with Sysmon/Windows Event Logs and LOLDrivers hash matching
- HVCI/WDAC vulnerable driver block list coverage gaps and operational implications

Use Cases:
- Terminate EDR/AV agents (including PPL) prior to payload deployment (ransomware-style defense evasion)
- Triage and weaponize signed drivers by identifying ungated IOCTL primitives at scale
- Build PoCs that interact with vulnerable drivers using Win32 APIs or Python `ctypes`
- Blue-team hunts for BYOVD activity using driver-load + service-creation + device-IO telemetry correlation
- Validate effectiveness of HVCI/WDAC policies against known-vulnerable driver inventories

Keywords:
BYOVD, IOCTL, DeviceIoControl, IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL, CTL_CODE, BootRepair.sys, LOLDrivers, DrvEye, Sysmon Event ID 6, Windows Event ID 7045, WDAC, Vulnerable Driver Block List, HVCI, Memory Integrity, DSE, PPL, ObOpenObjectByPointer, PsLookupProcessByProcessId, ZwTerminateProcess